  "summary": "Six preprints tie H&E models to molecular or cell-level truth and test cross-scanner and cross-dataset robustness, shifting evaluation beyond headline accuracy.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is grounding H&E-based models in molecular or cell-level reference data and testing whether outputs hold across acquisition conditions. HERMES uses a process reward that checks stated quantities against cell-level truth from paired mIF. VirTues-M2 pretrains jointly on morphology and spatial proteomics. XenoType supervises cell-type classification with Xenium and scRNA-seq references. The DNA damage response study predicts RPPA protein signaling from whole-slide images. On robustness, the tempering study targets slide-scanner dependence of digital biomarkers, and the white blood cell pipeline reports cross-dataset macro-F1 of 0.879. New here is the pairing of molecular supervision with explicit reproducibility checks. All items are preprints, so results remain provisional.",
